The main aim of data preprocessing is to convert the original dataset into an encoded dataframe called indra_chowk.

#Accessing each symptoms using their row index and column name:
first_list = [ ]
symptoms_dataframe = data_set.drop('Disease',axis=1)
symptoms_dataframe = symptoms_dataframe.fillna('None')
for row_index in symptoms_dataframe.index:
  for column_name in symptoms_dataframe.columns:
    cell_value = symptoms_dataframe.loc[row_index,column_name]
    cell_value = cell_value.strip()
    if(cell_value != 'None'):
      first_list.append((row_index,column_name,cell_value))
print(first_list)

#Making the foundational data for the encoded symptoms dataframe
stacked_dataframe = pd.DataFrame(first_list)
display(stacked_dataframe)

# A fourth column full of 1 is added as that '1' will be the cell value in the encoded symptoms dataframe.
#This is where the real encoding happens
stacked_dataframe[3] = 1
display(stacked_dataframe)


# pivot_table() is literally responsible for designing the encoded symptoms dataframe. It asks for the position of the columns in stacked_dataframe
where the index, column names and values are stored. 

# Surprisingly, the symptom columns are in alphabetical order.

# The actual encoded symptoms dataframe
#This is encoding 1
indra_chowk = pd.pivot_table(data=stacked_dataframe,index=0,columns=2,values=3,aggfunc='mean')
#This is encoding 0
indra_chowk = indra_chowk.fillna('0')
indra_chowk = indra_chowk.astype(int)
display(indra_chowk)


# Disease encoding
disease = data_set['Disease']
from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder
disease_encoder = LabelEncoder()
encoding_disease = disease_encoder.fit_transform(disease)
encoded_disease = pd.Series(encoding_disease)
display(encoded_disease)


#Inserting the encoded disease column into the encoded symptoms dataframe, finally forming an encoded dataset
indra_chowk.insert(loc=0,column='Disease',value=encoded_disease)
display(indra_chowk)

#Now, indra_chowk is a fully fledged encoded dataset.

#The whole point of designing this encoded dataset is so that it can provide training data which trains the classifiers to diagnose
on the symptoms sent by Gemini-3.5-flash.

#In the previous method, I had splitted on the basis of applying Group ID in order to avoid data leakage problem and used the testing data
for the classifiers to diagnose on. But, in this case, the classifiers are diagnosing on real-world symptoms and not testing symptoms from 
the encoded dataset. So, the testing data is basically useless in this case.

# For a classifier running on patient reported symptoms, splitting is absolutely unnecessary. The encoded dataset becomes the training data 
itself. The entire dataset trains the classifiers.

